<!-- Phase 9 website teaching layer; original chapter remains read-only. Evidence: website/reviews/android-phase9-findings.md. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">commonTest is green on the Android host, while the Apple consumer fails to import the framework. The test source was shared; the runtime and consumer were not exercised. Name the public contract, then name the target and host that can supply its evidence.</p>

<!-- @after 1. Understand What commonTest Means :: visual -->
### Read the test report as a matrix

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-grid" data-cols="2"><div class="node"><strong>Common source</strong><small>Reusable test intent and assertions.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Target execution</strong><small>A concrete compilation, runner and runtime.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Platform adapter</strong><small>The actual storage, networking or lifecycle boundary.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Native consumer</strong><small>The API after framework export, linking and Swift import.</small></div></div><figcaption>Record which tasks ran; a missing target report is missing evidence, not an implicit pass.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after 4. Test HTTP Contracts With Ktor MockEngine :: animation -->
### A fake failure proves a bounded contract

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">The repository falls back only for its declared recoverable failure</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="node" data-el="api" data-art="cloud" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Controlled API</strong><small data-text="apiV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="store" data-art="database" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Controlled store</strong><small data-text="storeV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="repo" data-art="code" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Repository</strong><small data-text="repoV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="assert" data-art="inspect" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Assertions</strong><small data-text="assertV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{
"label": "Arrange",
"caption": "Seed a cached item and configure the API’s recoverable transport failure.",
"state": {
"api": "warn",
"store": "done"
},
"text": {
"apiV": "recoverable failure",
"storeV": "cached item A",
"repoV": "not invoked",
"assertV": "pending"
},
"add": [
"api",
"store",
"repo",
"assert"
]
},
{
"label": "Call public API",
"caption": "Invoke the repository through the contract the product uses.",
"state": {
"repo": "active"
},
"text": {
"repoV": "requests category X"
}
},
{
"label": "Observe fallback",
"caption": "The declared failure takes the cached path in this example; assert the result and relevant request parameters.",
"state": {
"assert": "active"
},
"text": {
"assertV": "cached A; requested X"
}
},
{
"label": "Test a different boundary",
"caption": "Cancellation and unexpected defects are separate cases. Real transport and Apple export require separate execution evidence.",
"state": {
"assert": "done"
},
"text": {
"assertV": "scope of confidence recorded"
}
}
]</script><figcaption>The original repository catches its named transport exception. This model does not turn arbitrary failures or cancellation into success.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Common Failure Modes :: check -->
### Try the decision

<details><summary>commonTest exists. Has iOS run it?</summary>

Only the invoked target task and its report establish that. Require the Apple-capable job and platform-specific checks your support promise needs.
</details>

<details><summary>MockEngine decodes the payload. Has TLS been verified?</summary>

No. It uses no socket. Exercise the selected transport and real contract at an appropriate separate boundary.
</details>

<details><summary>Virtual time controls one dispatcher. Can another real dispatcher still wait?</summary>

Yes. Share the test scheduler among injected test dispatchers and identify work that intentionally runs outside it.
</details>
